, and known in ancient times as Brahman, there lived an old woman who owned a cow. She would take the cow out to graze in the forest every day. She noticed that the cow would go and stand at a particular spot in the Devtri cave on the bank of the Sinholi river, and that milk would flow spontaneously from its udder, anointing that spot. In the village of Varman lived a merchant named Dhandhal, a devout Jain and a shrewd trader. One night, the presiding deity told him in a dream that 2,800 years earlier, when Parshwanath was alive, King Chandrayash had had an image of Lord Parshwanath made. This image was extremely powerful. It lay buried beneath a tree in a cave outside the village of Varman, and the fact that a cow anointed that spot daily with milk out of devotion was a sign of the presence of this beautiful Jina image there. The merchant was instructed to bring the image out and to build and consecrate a temple for it.. The next morning, following the dream, he went outside the village and saw a cow standing beneath a tree, milk flowing spontaneously from its udder and anointing that very spot. Realizing this, he retrieved the image from that place. The sangh of the village of Brahman gathered for the revelation of this image, and many people from the nearby village of Jirapalli also came. The beautiful image of Lord Parshwa emerged from the earth, and all who beheld the Lord's incomparable beauty were overcome with devotion. Both the sanghs of Brahman and Jirapalli villages laid claim to this image — a dispute arose over which village would take it. This dispute was resolved by an elderly man: one bull was brought from each of the two places, and they were yoked to a cart. The Lord was placed in the cart, which then moved off in the direction of Jirapalli, coming to a stop near the foot of a hill in Jirapalli. A grand ceremony of installation was held in Jirapalli. In the temple of Shri Virprabhu, the place of the Mulanayak was given to this newly revealed image of the Lord. The Lord's manifestation occurred in Vikram Samvat 1109. Sri Dhandhal Shreshthi built a magnificent, towering temple. In this new shrine, in Vikram Samvat 1191, the revered Acharya Shri Ajitdevsuriji of Mandar enthroned the Lord. Acharya Shri Ajitdevsuriji declared that since this image had been made during the very lifetime of Lord Parshwanath, it was exceptionally powerful, and its glory would spread in all directions — that wherever a Jina temple's consecration would take place, the protective mantra of Lord Jiravala Parshwanath would always be inscribed there first. Not only in India but anywhere in the world where the consecration of a Jineshwar temple is duly performed, the mantra-name of Shri Jiravala Parshwanath — \\\"Om Hrim Shri Shri Jiravala Parshwanath Raksham Kuru Kuru Swaha\\\" — is inscribed on the wall behind the image. Time and circumstance are strange: as time passed, the Jain population of Jiravala dwindled, yet the influence and glory of Lord Jiravala Parshwanath remain unbroken even today.. This temple has undergone numerous renovations. The influence of this pilgrimage site, which fulfils the heartfelt wishes of devotees, grew rapidly. In Samvat 1368, the army of Alauddin killed Kanhaddev and conquered Jalore. During this invasion, Jiravala Tirth suffered heavy damage. The invaders had to bear the terrible consequences of their misdeeds. Due to this act, trouble (upsarga) befell the province of Jalore. After consulting his diwan, the governor came to the temple, begged forgiveness before the image, and had his head shaved, whereby he was freed of the affliction. It is said that the practice of head-shaving in this region began from this incident.. In the fifteenth century, Sanghvi Shri Alharaj, minister to Badshah Alamshah of Mandavgadh and son of Shri Jhanjhan Shreshthi, devoted his wealth to this pilgrimage site, making it permanently endowed. The lofty torans (archways), grand pillars, and beautiful canopy (chandarva) adorning the assembly hall (rangmandap) further enhanced the beauty of this Jina temple.. To protect this image from Muslim invasions, the Shri Sangh acted with great foresight. On the left side of the outer wall of the sanctum, images of Shri Jiravala Parshwanath and Dada Parshwanath were installed, while the position of Mulanayak was given to Lord Neminath. It cannot be said with certainty when this change took place.. In the present day, the influence of this pilgrimage site is known throughout the world. Devotees experience wondrous miracles at this sacred site. On all auspicious occasions such as consecrations and shantisnatra ceremonies everywhere, the mantra-syllables of Shri Jiravala Parshwanathji are inscribed — a true testament to the Lord's power.. Devout Jains recall and pay homage to this pilgrimage site every morning and evening in the tirtha-vandana sutra recited during pratikraman. Every year a fair is held here on Kartik Purnima, Chaitra Purnima, and the fifth day of the bright half of Bhadrapad.. Inscriptions at Arbudachal (Mount Abu) also prove that Shraman Bhagwan Shri Mahavir himself set foot upon Arbudachal. During this period, Jirapalli Tirth had already gained renown as a fully consecrated pilgrimage site. The Sakal Tirth Vandana Stotra also glorifies this pilgrimage site with the line \\\"Jiravalo ne Thambhan Pas.\\\". Various inscriptions, dedicatory prashastis, and colophons (pushpikas) provide evidence that this great pilgrimage site underwent renovation and re-consecration over many centuries. Renovations and consecrations took place in the 4th, 8th, 12th, and 16th centuries of the Vikram era, and strong evidence exists of extensive renovation and construction work in the 18th and 19th centuries as well.. A major recurring cause for these repeated renovations was the Mughal invasions. Since the Marudhar region formed the western frontier of Aryavarta, our pilgrimage sites and temples were the first to suffer damage from the attacks of fanatical foreign powers. These zealots demolished temples and defaced images, but they could never destroy the faith rooted in people's hearts. As a result, the pilgrimage site was rebuilt again and again through the selfless dedication of the faithful. Following the wise counsel of the earlier acharyas, our ancestors combined whatever limited means were available to them with their faith, undertaking a Herculean effort to preserve intact the glory of the Lord's image and his abode. It is by virtue of that effort that we are blessed with the presence of this deity even today.. Other Temples: There is one other temple.. Art and Sculpture: The natural scenery of this secluded spot amid the hills in the forest is extremely beautiful, peaceful, and captivating.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/S4R6rZfd8nbTctN96\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-ui text-sm font-medium text-ivory hover:bg-maroon-deep transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":\\\"$8:2:props:children:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:iconNode\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-map-pin h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],\\\"Open in Google Maps\\\"]}],null,[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L20\\\",null,{\\\"text\\\":\\\"$21\\\",\\\"lang\\\":\\\"en\\\",\\\"label\\\":\\\"Listen\\\",\\\"stopLabel\\\":\\\"Stop\\\"}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-10 grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 sm:grid-cols-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"35c16b36-215e-4808-8a49-71e307d79194\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 col-span-2 aspec